# HTML / XHTML parsing (Node.js)

Use **htmlparser2** (and **`DomUtils`** from the same ecosystem) to parse HTML or XHTML in server-side code. **Do not** use regular expressions to walk tags, attributes, or document structure — regex is brittle for nested markup and easy to get wrong on attribute order, namespaces, and EPUB quirks.

## When this applies

- EPUB chapter XHTML, fragments from blobs, or any non-trivial markup extraction in Node (e.g. `@reading/jobs`, `@reading/epub-utils`).
- Classifying pages (cover / TOC / body), reading `<meta>`, `<title>`, headings, or plain text from subtrees.

## Preferred APIs

- **`parseDocument(data, { xmlMode: true })`** for full XHTML documents (EPUB content documents).
- **`DomUtils.findOne` / `DomUtils.findAll`** with predicates on `Element` nodes (tag name, attributes).
- **`DomUtils.textContent(node)`** for visible text inside an element or subtree.
- **`DomUtils.getInnerHTML`** / **`getElementsByTagName`** when you need legacy-style helpers.

For **streaming** or very large inputs without building a full DOM, use **`Parser`** from `htmlparser2` with callbacks (see `packages/epub-utils/src/text-extractor.ts`).

## Dependencies

- Add **`htmlparser2`** (and **`domhandler`** for `Document` / `Element` types) to the package that performs parsing; align versions with `packages/epub-utils` where practical.

## Avoid

- Regex for structural HTML/XML extraction (`<body>...</body>`, heading lists, meta tags).
- **`jsdom`** unless you need a full browser DOM — it is heavier than needed for pipeline-style parsing.

```typescript
// ✅ Prefer: DOM from htmlparser2 + DomUtils
import { parseDocument, DomUtils } from 'htmlparser2';
import type { Document, Element } from 'domhandler';

const doc = parseDocument(xhtml, { xmlMode: true });
const body = DomUtils.findOne(
	(el): el is Element => DomUtils.isTag(el) && el.name === 'body',
	doc,
	true,
);

// ❌ Avoid: regex as a “parser” for structure
const inner = xhtml.match(/<body\b[^>]*>([\s\S]*?)<\/body>/i);
```
